<p>Give it the &quot;meta&quot; tag, because this is a meta skip.</p>
<p>This is the part I have a problem with especially:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>There's no object with special containment procedures, <strong>or even an anomaly</strong>.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><a href="/scp-765">SCP-765</a> is heavily implied to be non-anomalous. <a href="/scp-1949">SCP-1949</a> has no spelled-out anomaly (and I spent so long ranting about that SCP that I had to excuse myself from the site for a few weeks), neither does:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/scp-257-arc">SCP-257-ARC</a>, aka the origin of the phrase &quot;Does the Black Moon howl?&quot; Technically gone from the site, but the fact that it's an archived skip implies it has some value.</li>
<li><a href="/scp-579">SCP-579</a>, whose description just reads [DATA EXPUNGED], and we're told it's anomalous, but now how it's anomalous.</li>
<li><a href="/scp-2864">SCP-2864</a>, which literally has a bunch of researchers notes about whether or not it's anomalous and no proper skip designation?</li>
</ul>
<p>Not to mention: this is sitting at +22. Not a great rating, but not likely to get downvoted to oblivion any time in the next week, at least. I don't see the technical team posting on whether or not <a href="/scp-2763">SCP-2763</a> qualifies as a skip or not, and that has even less of a skip-like format than this does.</p>
<p>Why does this deserve to be designated as &quot;not a skip&quot;, exactly? It has containment procedures, and having an explicit anomaly (or an anomaly at all) is optional, and this can, at the very least, be given the &quot;meta&quot; tag, and if not the &quot;thaumiel&quot; tag, then at least &quot;unclassed&quot;. So, I ask again: why does this deserve special scrutiny?</p>

