<p>They key component every example you've cited has is thheir feasibility in being an actual entry in the Foundation database, that the Mainlist is supposed to represent. 765 and 1949 are standard format with unorthodox concepts. 579 and 257-ARC feature heavy expungment that isn't unusual for documentation (and much more common for the period they were written in): 257-ARC clearly is a skull, and 579 most definitely follows format while being abstract as <a href="/scp-2719">SCP-2719</a>. 2763 and 2864 fall under &quot;anomalies affecting their documentation&quot;, which has been accepted many times before.</p>
<p>However, this article isn't doing any of the above. It's a selection of internal communication between employees, and not something one would expect to show up on the &quot;database&quot; at all. There's a PROPOSAL for an article, but the sequential messages imply that the proposal doesn't even hold weight as an actual document, let alone anomaly.</p>
<p>It would have been better to cite shaggy or Bright's 001 proposals as an example, which feature literal story-telling on what's supposed to be a mockup of one of the Foundation's most coveted secrets. Even then, they're 001 proposals and are <em>expected</em> to be boundary-breaking,</p>
<p>The votes do speak for themselves here, though it does show how far we're reaching just for original takes on execution. I'm thinking this needs to be discussed by staff before officially labeling this as a tale&#8212; I haven't even seen input from <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/captain-kirby" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3440103); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=3440103&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1513512222" alt="Captain Kirby" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=3440103)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/captain-kirby" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3440103); return false;">Captain Kirby</a></span> yet.</p>

